# Fix issue #364

This PR adds two new methods to the `GoogleAIFileManager` class:

- **`uploadFileFromBuffer`**: Uploads a file using a Buffer or ArrayBuffer. This method works in both Node.js and serverless environments.
- **`uploadFileFromUrl`**: Uploads a file directly from a given URL by first fetching the file data.

These changes provide more flexibility in handling file uploads, improve error handling, and ensure backward compatibility with the original `uploadFile` method.

## Usage Example

```typescript
import { readFile } from "fs/promises";
import { GoogleAIFileManager } from "./file-manager";

// Instantiate the file manager with your API key
const fileManager = new GoogleAIFileManager("your-api-key");

// --- Upload using a Buffer ---
async function uploadFromBuffer(): Promise<void> {
  // Read a file from the local file system
  const fileBuffer = await readFile("./path/to/your/image.png");

  // Upload the file using the Buffer method
  const bufferResponse = await fileManager.uploadFileFromBuffer(fileBuffer, {
    mimeType: "image/png",
    displayName: "example-buffer-upload",
  });

  console.log("Buffer upload result:", bufferResponse.file.uri);
}

// --- Upload using a URL ---
async function uploadFromUrl(): Promise<void> {
  const fileUrl = "https://example.com/your-image.png";

  // Upload the file using the URL method
  const urlResponse = await fileManager.uploadFileFromUrl(fileUrl, {
    mimeType: "image/png",
    displayName: "example-url-upload",
  });

  console.log("URL upload result:", urlResponse.file.uri);
}

// Execute both examples
(async () => {
  await uploadFromBuffer();
  await uploadFromUrl();
})();
```
### Additional Notes
1. The new methods delegate to a common upload implementation to maintain consistency.
2. Proper error handling is in place to manage network or file system issues.
3. These changes are backward compatible with the original uploadFile method.

This PR implements a more generic `ddBody` method to the `Illuminate/Testing/TestResponse` class which is useful for dumping out any HTTP response body. 

The `ddJson` method was added previously and this code will use that if `json_validate` passes. Otherwise it will dump out the content of the response.

I noticed there weren't any tests for the other methods so I didn't write a test for this, however happy to write tests for this if needed.

